    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before European settlement, the land that became Anson was home to the Norridgewock Abenaki people.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anson%2C_Maine",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Anson was incorporated on March 1, 1798, and named after Lord George Anson.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anson%2C_Maine",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1886, Anson produced boots, shoes, leather, bricks, lumber, flour, and wool rolls, powered by two sawmills and three factories.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anson%2C_Maine",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "North Anson became the northern terminus of the Somerset Railroad in 1876, the line reopened in 2007, then abandoned again in 2013.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        13,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anson%2C_Maine",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Anson’s landscape is shaped by Mill Stream, Gilbert Brook, Lemon Stream, the Carrabassett River, and the Kennebec River.",
